    - A call with a path returns the ancestor chain FIRST — every .aide spec
      from root down to the target leaf, each with its description field —
      then the detailed subtree of that directory with summaries and anomaly
      warnings. The ancestor chain is not a separate mode or flag; it is
      always part of a path-scoped response.
    - "Each node in the ancestor chain shows: the spec path, the description
      field value, and the status field if set to aligned or misaligned.
      An agent reading the chain knows what every ancestor intends and
      whether alignment has been verified, without opening any of them."
    - "Specs with status: misaligned are surfaced inline with a visible flag
      so any agent walking the tree notices drift immediately, without
      needing to call a separate validation tool."
    - The tool's description and output shape teach agents the cascading
      intent protocol by example — agents learn "walk ancestors, then drill
      subtree" from calling discover, not from paragraphs of instruction in
      their agent definitions.
    - Output is a single human-readable tree the agent can scan visually,
      with type tags, inline summaries only when present, and misalignment
      flags only when set.
  undesired:
    - A shallow call that reads any file contents. The whole point of the
      shallow mode is being cheap enough to call speculatively; reading
      files silently turns it into the deep mode and burns context for no
      benefit.
    - A path-scoped call that omits the ancestor chain, forcing the agent
      to make separate calls or manually walk up the tree to understand
      inherited context. The ancestor chain is the mechanism that makes
      cascading intent visible; omitting it defeats the purpose.
    - An ancestor chain that requires agents to open each ancestor spec to
      understand what it governs. The description field exists so the chain
      is self-contained — if an agent needs to aide_read an ancestor just
      to understand the chain, the description is missing or inadequate.
    - A deep call that walks the entire project instead of just the requested
      subtree. Drill-in is a focusing operation; widening it defeats the
      purpose.
    - Output that dumps summaries even when they are empty strings, producing
