## Context

discover is where an agent first meets the AIDE convention in practice.
Every other tool is called in response to something discover revealed: read
drills into a spec discover pointed at; scaffold creates into a gap discover
showed; validate acts on anomalies discover only partially reveals. If
discover gets its shape wrong, the whole workflow downstream of it pays the
cost. The discipline of this tool is making its two modes each earn their
runtime cost.

AIDE specs form a cascading intent tree: a root spec sets project-wide
intent, and every child narrows its parent. An agent working on a leaf
module inherits constraints from every ancestor between the root and that
leaf. Without discover surfacing that lineage, the agent must manually walk
upward through the filesystem, opening specs one at a time, to reconstruct
the context it inherits. The ancestor chain solves this by returning the
full lineage in a single call — each node carrying its description and
alignment status — so inherited intent is visible before the agent reads a
single spec body.

The description and status fields are lightweight metadata that make the
ancestor chain self-contained. A description is a one-liner capturing a
spec's purpose; an agent reading it knows what an ancestor governs without
opening it. A status tracks alignment state: pending (default, implicit),
aligned (verified by the aligner), or misaligned (drift detected by QA).
Together they let discover's output carry enough signal that the agent can
decide where to focus without additional reads.

## Strategy

**Two modes, one switch: the presence of `path`.** No path means a
shallow project-wide map — locations and type tags only, no content
inspected. With a path means a deep subtree scan — ancestor chain first,
then summaries and anomaly warnings drawn from the files in that subtree.
The switch is not hidden behind a flag; `path` is the switch, because
that's the same field the agent is already using to scope its attention.

**Shallow is cheap on purpose.** The shallow mode exists so an agent can
call discover at the start of every session without worrying about context
burn. Reading file contents in the shallow mode would turn a cheap,
predictable map into an unpredictable expense and force agents to guess
when it's "safe" to call. The cheapness is the feature. The shallow mode
does not surface ancestor chains, descriptions, or status — those require
reading frontmatter, which violates the no-content-read constraint.

**Deep mode returns the ancestor chain before the subtree.** When a path
is provided, the response opens with the ancestor chain — every spec from
root down to the target, each showing its path, description field value,
and status if non-default. The chain appears before the subtree detail
because inherited context must be established before the agent interprets
the subtree's own specs. This is not a separate mode or optional flag; it
is always part of a path-scoped response, because a subtree without its
lineage is a leaf without a branch.

**The ancestor chain walks up, then renders down.** Implementation walks
from the target path upward through the filesystem to the project root,
collecting every .aide spec it finds at each directory level. The output
renders them top-down — root first, target last — because that matches the
order an agent needs to read inherited intent: broadest context first,
narrowing toward the module it is about to work on.

**Description makes the chain self-contained.** Each ancestor node
displays its description field — a one-line purpose statement from
frontmatter. An agent reading the chain should understand what every
ancestor governs without needing to aide_read any of them. If a spec
lacks a description field, the chain shows the path with no description
rather than fabricating one from the intent paragraph or summary. Missing
descriptions are a signal that the spec needs updating, not a gap for the
tool to paper over.

**Status is shown only when non-default.** The three states are pending
(implicit default — spec exists but alignment has not been reviewed),
aligned (aligner confirmed), and misaligned (QA flagged drift). Pending
is the absence of a status field and is not rendered — showing "pending"
on every unreviewed spec would be noise. Aligned and misaligned are shown
inline next to the spec path. Misaligned specs get a visible flag so drift
is noticed passively by any agent walking the tree, without requiring a
separate validation call.

**Misalignment is informational, not blocking.** A misaligned flag in the
ancestor chain tells the agent that drift has been detected at that level.
It does not halt the pipeline, reject the call, or force remediation. The
agent can report it, factor it into its work, or note it for a later QA
pass. Making it blocking would turn every discover call into a gate check
and discourage agents from calling discover speculatively.

**Deep is content-aware on purpose.** When the agent has already decided
where to focus, summaries and anomaly warnings earn their cost. The agent
is paying for context because it's about to spend context on that subtree
anyway — summaries let it pick the right spec to read first, and anomaly
warnings surface problems exactly where the agent is about to make
changes.

**Let the tool's own behavior teach the pattern.** An agent reading the
description for discover learns "call with no path for the map, call with
a path to drill in and see your ancestor chain" — and that pattern is
cascading intent through progressive disclosure. The agent internalizes
both shapes from discover's output: the flat map for orientation, and the
ancestors-then-subtree structure for focused work. This is the only tool
in the server whose *behavior* is part of what it teaches.

**Render the tree for human eyes, not program parsing.** Unicode
box-drawing characters, alphabetical sort, one leaf per line, type tag in
brackets, summary after an em-dash only when present. An empty summary
collapses to "filename [type]" with no trailing punctuation. The ancestor
chain uses a distinct visual form — a vertical lineage list, not a tree
— so agents can distinguish "these are your inherited ancestors" from
"these are the specs in the subtree you asked about." Status badges
appear inline after the description when set.

## Good examples

An agent starts a session in an unfamiliar project and calls `aide_discover`
with no arguments. The response is:

> src/
> ├── service/
> │   ├── notifications/
> │   │   ├── .aide [intent]
> │   │   └── retryPolicy/
> │   │       └── .aide [intent]
> │   └── orders/
> │       ├── research.aide [research]
> │       ├── intent.aide [intent]
> │       └── todo.aide [todo]

Five files, one tree, no summaries, no warnings, no ancestor chains, no
content reads. The agent now knows the project's spec architecture and
decides to drill into `src/service/orders` next.

The agent then calls `aide_discover` with path `src/service/orders`. The
response opens with the ancestor chain, then the subtree detail:

> Ancestor chain:
>   .aide/intent.aide — Canonical home of the AIDE methodology and its MCP delivery server
>   src/.aide — MCP server implementing the two delivery channels [aligned]
>   src/service/.aide — Service modules that compose the business logic layer
>
> src/service/orders/
> ├── research.aide [research] — Synthesis of order-completion patterns across common e-commerce platforms
> ├── intent.aide [intent] — Create orders from validated payloads and emit confirmations
> └── todo.aide [todo] — 3 unresolved issues from latest audit run

The agent sees three things before it reads a single spec body: (1) the
project exists to deliver AIDE through an MCP server, (2) the src layer
is about delivery channels and has been verified as aligned, (3) the
service layer composes business logic. It now has the full inherited
context for the orders subtree and can pick which spec to read first.

An agent drills into a deeply nested module and sees a misaligned ancestor:

> Ancestor chain:
>   .aide/intent.aide — Lead intelligence platform for local service businesses
>   src/.aide — Data pipeline orchestration and API surface [aligned]
>   src/service/.aide — Service modules for scoring, enrichment, and outreach [misaligned]
>   src/service/scoring/.aide — Multi-signal lead scoring engine
>
> src/service/scoring/
> ├── .aide [intent] — Score leads using weighted digital presence signals
> └── plan.aide [plan] — 4 of 7 steps completed

The agent notices that `src/service/.aide` is flagged misaligned. It can
choose to report this finding, factor the drift into its work, or note it
for a later QA pass — but it is not blocked from proceeding. The flag did
its job: drift is visible without a separate validation call.

## Bad examples

A shallow call that returns this:

> src/
> ├── service/
> │   ├── notifications/
> │   │   ├── .aide [intent] — This module routes notifications through a priority-based queue...

The shallow mode has silently read file contents. It's no longer cheap; it
no longer behaves the way the description promised; and the agent that
called it speculatively just spent context it didn't mean to spend.

A path-scoped call that returns the subtree detail but no ancestor chain:

> src/service/orders/
> ├── research.aide [research] — Synthesis of order-completion patterns
> ├── intent.aide [intent] — Create orders from validated payloads
> └── todo.aide [todo] — 3 unresolved issues

The agent can see what's in the orders subtree but has no idea what the
project, src, or service layers intend. It must now make separate calls or
manually walk upward to reconstruct the inherited context. The ancestor
chain exists to prevent exactly this — omitting it forces the agent into
the manual tree-walk the tool was built to eliminate.

An ancestor chain that shows paths but no descriptions:

> Ancestor chain:
>   .aide/intent.aide
>   src/.aide
>   src/service/.aide

The agent sees the lineage but learns nothing from it. To understand what
each ancestor governs, it must aide_read all three — turning a single
discover call into four calls. The description field exists to make the
chain self-contained; omitting descriptions defeats that purpose.

An ancestor chain that shows "pending" on every spec that has no status:

> Ancestor chain:
>   .aide/intent.aide — Canonical AIDE methodology [pending]
>   src/.aide — MCP server delivery channels [pending]
>   src/service/.aide — Service modules [pending]

Pending is the default — the absence of a review, not a finding. Showing
it on every unreviewed spec drowns out the specs that actually have a
meaningful status. Aligned and misaligned are signals; pending is noise.

A deep call scoped to the orders subtree that walks the full `src/`
tree anyway, because "the user probably wanted the whole project." Drill-in
is a focusing operation; widening it ignores the agent's explicit scope
request and turns every deep call into the worst of both modes.

A misaligned flag that blocks the discover response or returns an error:

> Error: ancestor src/service/.aide is misaligned. Resolve before proceeding.

Misalignment is informational. The flag exists so agents notice drift; it
does not exist to gate their work. Making it blocking discourages calling
discover speculatively and turns a map-making tool into a validator.

A tree where summaries were extracted as empty strings but still printed:

> ├── .aide [intent] —
> ├── intent.aide [intent] —

The trailing em-dashes tell the agent nothing and waste the visual space
that would otherwise help it spot the meaningful entries.

## References

No external research notes were used. All context was drawn from:
- `.aide/intent.aide` -- project-level cascading intent model and single-source-of-truth invariant that discover must surface
- `src/.aide` -- delivery channel architecture and the teaching-through-tooling constraint that shapes discover's output
- `src/tools/discover/index.ts` -- current two-mode implementation confirming the path-as-switch pattern and shallow/deep split
- `src/tools/discover/buildTree/index.ts` -- current tree rendering approach: type-sorted, box-drawing, summary-after-em-dash convention
